From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Jun 20 07:43:36 2000
From: Holger Bauer <bauer () itsm ! uni-stuttgart ! de>
Date: Tue, 20 Jun 2000 07:43:36 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Fortran 90 and check_bounds
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96148706719457

The following message is a courtesy copy of an article
that has been posted to comp.lang.fortran as well.

Suppose the following code (with NB(L)=0)

     ....(L,MAXL,NB,IBC)
INTEGER :: NB(MAXL),IBC(6)
IF(NB(L).GT.0.AND.IBC(L).EQ.3)THEN
...
ELSE
...
ENDIF

The code does NOT run on Tru64Unix and SGI IRIX64 if the
check_bounds option is set AND NB(L) is equal to zero.
(using 5.3 Version of the Compaq compiler)

It is clear to me, that 0 then is NOT a valid subscript for
the array IBC (IBC(0)). However, my understanding of Fortran
(and other languages) was that logical expressions with the
same prioriy are parsed from left to right and the compiler
will NOT evaluate the remaining expressions once the whole
expression will already be .FALSE. (as it will be with the
NB(L).GT.0.AND. subexpression.

Since we have many of these expressions in our code (and
this was NEVER a problem with F77 with check_bounds) I ask
myself if this is a compiler bug or this is according to the
F90 specifications.

Regards,

Holger

