The suggestion that was made by most who responded was to make device
tz12, this was done as follows:

rm rm* and rm nrm* - to remove the old devices
./MAKEDEV tz12 - tz12 from second controller x8 + unit number - 1*8+4=12

scu scan edt
scu show edt , although it would only find the new device after a reboot,
which showed at console level and when rebooting

next was to mount the device and try a command

mt -f /dev/nrmt0h online - this worked, loading the tape and bringing it
online
mt -f /dev/nrmt0h 'any command' this failed reporting I/O ERROR

There was also mention of a firmware issue, but after consulting a local
DEC engineer we are told there are no records of any firmware problems
associated with the TSZ07.

We also tried booting genvmunix and running doconfig, but this didn't work
either. Dr Blinn believes this is simply a case of not all devices can be
expected to work with all cards with all versions of Tru-unix,
particularly with older devices.

We then tried installing the TSZ07 on a scsi pc and this worked fine.  We
can write to the device with appropriate shareware and read files off the
tape back to the pc.  However we could not discover a way to write files
and/or tape headers that can be read by the PDP's.

 After going over the steps detailed above a couple of times on several
different alphas having different scsi cards and still failing we will try
building a VMS system on an old vax3100 which we found 'upstairs'. The
original vax which we got to work is no longer available.

stuart mckenzie


original:
Subject: TSZ07 on Alpha Unix


A few weeks ago we had an urgent requirement for getting files from an
Alpha to a RSTS system.  We summarised by saying that we had borrowed a 9
track TSZ07 tape drive from a helpful site near us and had loaded the file
onto 9track and thence onto the PDP11.

It now transpires that we did this using VMS which will not be available
to us much longer.  Apparently it failed under UNIX.  It worked under VMS
and also on a scsi based pc but not on the alpha.

Nobody likes to be beaten by this sort of problem so even though the
urgency has gone (and we are still organising the Kermit route), we would
like to crack this problem.

What happens is that, regardless of which type of alpha scsi interface we
try, when the special device files are built (and after the previous ones
were removed for safety), you are able to address the drive and get it to
go online, but anything else you try to do generates an 'I/O error'
reported in the binary error log as a CAM scsi error.

We thought we had seen this in the archives and sure enough Miguel Fliguer
reported this problem last year and in his summary said it was firmware.
Unfortunately he does not seem to be at the same location as emails to him
do not get a reply.  Compaq here state absolutely that there is NO
firmware issue and that it must be a scsi config/unix problem.

We thought it might be us configging wrong so would appreciate any extra
views you may have:

For example it is currently on an external scsi plug showing at SRM as
MKB400.
We assume that means we
rm /dev/nrm*  /dev/rm* 
/dev/MAKEDEV tz84


and address the drive so:

mt -f /dev/nrmt1h  online
mt -f /dev/nrmt1h  status


In case we counted the scsi buses incorrectly we also tried making tz164
but no difference, we still get an I/O error other than for the online
command.

Any offers?
stuart mckenzie

