From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jun 16 20:30:13 2000
From: Darryl Milczarek <darryl.milczarek () emsusa ! com>
Date: Fri, 16 Jun 2000 20:30:13 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Summary: Pin an  executable into Unix memory - part II
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96118722923960

A couple more last minute comments just in:

alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com
	One extra thing the others didn't mention is the sticky
	bit.  The original intent from back in the days of UNIX
	running on PDP-11 without paging, was that parts of the
	program would continue to reside in memory after the
	last exited when the sticky bit was on.  While a program
	was still running the text and read-data pretending to
	be text would be shared and resident.

	The sticky bit doesn't have the same meaning in the brave
	new world of demand paged virtual memory (said with tongue
	firmly in cheek), it may still have some affect, which
	improve the page-in time when that's needed.

Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-884-0646 [tpb@doctor.zk3.dec.com]
     It is a common misconception that an MFS is guaranteed to
     stay in memory.
     The utility that implements MFS does not lock itself in
     memory; it uses its own address space to create the MFS.
     If the pages are needed by the system, they get swapped out,
     just like any other program's pages.


The gist of my question was: "Is it possible to load an executable into
 memory in DEC Unix 4.0d so that it doesn't have to get read from disk
 every time it executes?"

Thanks again for the help,

Darryl Milczarek
darryl.milczarek@emsusa.com
EMS 602 258-9114 ext. 220
Mobile 602 818-0020

