Does anyone have experience managing a TL893 tape library
on a Tru64 Unix system? I am in the process of setting up
one of these things to use with Legato NetWorker on a DS20
running Tru64 Unix 4.0f. After many hours of effort, I am
pretty close to getting this setup to work. I can export
tapes out of the library into the IOD (i.e., load port),
I can move tapes within the tape library using the mcutil
and MRU GUI. I can even get Legato NetWorker to play nice
with it now. What I can't seem to figure out is how to take
tapes out of the thing in an automated manner. As I understand
it, the "nsrjb -d" command from NetWorker doesn't work with
this. The thing just sits there. 

When I try various forms of the mcutil command, I can get
the green light on the IOD unit to light up, I can put a
tape in through the load port on the IOD, the tape library
moves the tape into place, but the robotic arm never grabs
the tape to move it into a slot. I get the same results
if I use the "mcutil -m" command or if I drag the inport
icon to the stage icon or an empty slot icon in the MRU GUI.
The result is as I just described and in each case, I get
an error that says:

Move Medium failed on /dev/mc2: Vendor unique error.
SENSE KEY: 4, Asc 83, ASCQ: 2

I went through Compaq's web site where I got the Media Robot
Utility software from in search of documentation. I also installed
Netscape on the DS20 so I could read the documentation that came
in the software's tar file. Nothing there proves to be of any
use. Ditto with the online help for the MRU software, for scu,
and mcutil. I suspect I am just doing something wrong, or I still
have something misconfigured somewhere, but I have no idea what.
If anyone has any suggestions, please let me know.

From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jun 16 18:00:56 2000
From: "Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-884-0646" <tpb () doctor ! zk3 ! dec ! com>
Date: Fri, 16 Jun 2000 18:00:56 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Product release news -- Tru64 UNIX V4.0G is shipping
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96117851506725

For those of you still running V4.0D or a later V4.0x release, or  for
those of you planning to get one of the new GS series systems with a
V4.0 based release, we have good news..

Tru64 UNIX V4.0G has started shipping in the US.  If you order a new
V4.0x kit (using the QA-MT4AA-H8 part number) you will get V4.0G.  If
you have media or documentation update services, the new media kit or
documentation (or both, if you have both services) should be on the
way to you by now.

I am running V4.0G on my workstation and it's working very well.  If
you have not yet committed to move to V5.0A (or to V5.1 when it comes
out later this year), you should consider moving to V4.0G.  There is
no "right to new version" license fee if you are already licensed for
V4.0 (any letter).  All you need is the new media.

For those of you running Tru64 UNIX (or Digital UNIX) on a DEC 2000,
DEC 3000, DEC 4000, DEC 7000, or DEC 10000 system (all of which are
very old machines that lack the PCI option interface bus), you need
to be aware that Compaq plans to drop support for those systems in
a version of Tru64 UNIX in the V5.x release stream.  This has been
announced in the V5.0 and V5.0A release notes.  If you have not yet
decided to move to V5.0A with these older systems, you may want to
stay with V4.0x and move to V4.0G at your earliest opportunity.  The
V4.0G release does support the older systems, and it is like to have
a relatively long service life.

