From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jun 16 17:53:09 2000
From: Oisin McGuinness <oisin () sbcm ! com>
Date: Fri, 16 Jun 2000 17:53:09 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Question re change in tcptwreorder from 4.0D to 4.0F
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96117805806075



Dear Al:

We have an in-house TCP application which updates a Sybase database over both LAN and WAN
connections. Since a server in London was updated from 4.0D to 4.0F, the application
occasionally stalls, and builds up a backlog of data on the client side (in New York, the client has had
no recent changes; it is on an Ultrix4.3a box; the application has had no changes in 2 years),
and needs to be restarted to kick it back into update mode. (The application in question is
designed to stay up all the time; in practice it gets restarted as needed, but this is usually
very infrequently.)

Looking at various network configurations on the updated server, the only difference I see
between this box, and a similar 4.0D machine is that
the tcptwreorder variable (value visible in sysconfig -q inet), has changed from 1 in 4.0D
to 0 in 4.0F. (And the public domain netconfig program shows a change in max socket buffer size
(sb_max) size from 128K in 4.0D to 1M in 4.0F, but I don't think this matters.)

The explanation of this variable given in the System Tuning Guide is:

tcptwreorder 
     Enables (1) or disables (0) movement of TCP inpcbs in TIME_WAIT state to end of inpcb list. 

     Default value: 1 (enabled) 

Questions:

1. Can anyone say what the practical import of this variable change is?
2. And does anyone know why a change happened?

(By the way, I've looked in Stevens "TCP/IP Illustrated" Volume 2 for any related variables/code, but haven't found
anything directly useful; there is a discussion of manipulating the inpcb lists in Chapter 22, with
a mention on page 750 of a proposal to move pcb's around on the list under some
circumstances. But there is no mention of code related to TIME_WAIT state. Chapters 28 and 29
on tcp input seem possibly relevant, but I can't understand them sufficiently to tell.)

I don't know if this is responsible for the problem we are seeing or not, but I would
be grateful for responses.

Thanks in advance.

