X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96117535401186


I received considerable help from a few people.  I have collected the
suggestions below.  The solution appeared to be that the setuid bit
was not turned on for ssh1 on the remote machine.

Original Question:
=================

I have a user that is trying to slogin to the cluster from the cluster,
but it keeps asking for his passwd.  His .shosts file contains the names
of all the cluster members and the cluster alias.  He says he needs this
behavior to work in order to use his CVS (Control Version Swr) tree.

Does anyone have any ideas on this matter?
Thanks,
Lynn Bogovich
lynn@stsci.edu

Response 1:
===========
Lynn-

Are you using ssh-1.2.X or are using ssh-2.X or openssh ?

Does your /etc/ssh/sshd_config (or /etc/sshd_config) honor .shosts
authentication?  One alternative is for the user to set up an RSA
identity key and use ssh-agent.

Check your sshd_config file for things that will disallow .shosts
authentication:

IgnoreRhosts yes

or

DenySHosts or similar


I highly recommend the use of ssh-agent ... very convenient.

Here are some directions that I wrote up for a user (NOTE: we only
use xdm and csh (really tcsh), so if you are CDE shop and use ksh,
you'll need to poke around to add this to the users login scripts
or whatever):

On your remote machine:
0) cd ~/.ssh
1) ssh-keygen

the session will look something like:

% ssh-keygen
Initializing random number generator...
Generating p:  ...++ (distance 94)
Generating q:  .......................................++ (distance 596)
Computing the keys...
Testing the keys...
Key generation complete.
Enter file in which to save the key (/path/to/user/.ssh/identity): 

(NOTE: passphrase should be something reasonable ... you will only
       need to type it once when you log in ... make it real!)

Enter passphrase: 
Enter the same passphrase again:

Your identification has been saved in /path/to/user/.ssh/identity.
Your public key is:
1024 37 1040.... user@remote
Your public key has been saved in /path/to/user/.ssh/identity.pub

2) cp identity.pub authorized_keys
3) scp authorized_keys cluster:.ssh
4) ssh-agent
5) ssh-add

(it will ask you for your passphase that you set above)

6) scp identity* cluster:.ssh

(did it ask for your password?)

That's cool... but this is where the truly wondeful thing
happens... if we make your entire xsession a decendent of
ssh-agent, you will only need to enter your passphrase once
when you log onto the machine ...

7) cd
8) cp .xsession .xsession-real
9) make .xsession only contain:

Here is an example .xsession file for use with ssh-agent:

#!/bin/csh
#
# This is to set up ssh fun and games
set path = (/usr/bin /usr/local/bin)

ssh-agent csh ${HOME}/.xsession-real

9) add following lines to the top of .xsession-real

#
# Set up SSH business
# - this will add ~/.ssh/identity at start of X session
if ( -e /usr/local/bin/ssh-add1 || -e /usr/bin/ssh-add1 ) then
   ssh-add1 </dev/null
endif

