Dear Managers,

     The original post is at the end.  There were lots of responses!
I was given three locations for flex:

     ftp://ftp.gnu.org/non-gnu/flex/   (Missed that!)
     http://www.sunfreeware.com
     ftp://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/BSD/NetBSD/packages/distfiles/flex-2.5.4a.tar.gz

     Apparently flex is not covered by the GNU Public License, so it's
"non-gnu".

     The response on accounts was mixed.  Several people told me that
Solaris uses a different "crypt" than Tru64, so there is no shortcut
(because the Alpha chip is little-endian).  I received a couple of
recommendations to use a null password and force people to change
their passwords at first login (this worries me since at a college
during the summer some people don't log in frequently; accounts with
no password could be a target).  Several people claimed to have
scripts that do it; I've included one from Paul Yahnig which he
attributes to Michael Matthews.  Allen Carpenter recommended looking
at http://docs.sun.com.  I'll file a second summary on this when I
actually try it next week.

     I also received one warning from William Magill that Solaris is
considerably different than Tru64 and that I should prepare users for
this.  I expected that, but I have no management experience with
Solaris so I have to educate myself first.

Here's the script:

#!/usr/local/bin/perl

open(EDAUTH,"/usr/tcb/bin/edauth -d p -g |") || die "can't open edauth: $!\n";
while (<EDAUTH>) {
        /:u_name=([^:]+):/ && do {
                $prpname=$1;
        };
        /:u_pwd=([^:]+):/ && do {
                $prpasswords{$prpname} = $1;
        };
        /:u_lock:/ && do {
                $locked{$prpname} = 1;
        };
};
close(EDAUTH);

$filter = "root";

while (($name, $pwd, $uid, $gid, $quota, $comment, $gcos, $dir, $shell) =
        getpwent) {
        $tmpname = $name . ":";
        if ($tmpname !~ $filter) {
                $added++;
                $password = "*";
                $names{$name} = $name;
                $uids{$name} = $uid;
                $gids{$name} = $gid;
                $quotas{$name} = $quota;
                $comments{$name} = $comment;
                $gcosii{$name} = $gcos;
                $dirs{$name} = $dir;
                $shells{$name} = $shell;
                if (!$locked{$name}) {
                        $passwords{$name} = $prpasswords{$name};
                } else {
                        print "NOTICE:  ${name}'s account is locked\n";
                        $passwords{$name} = "*";
                };
        };
};

foreach $key (sort keys(%names)) {
        print "$key:$passwords{$key}:$uids{$key}:$gids{$key}:$comments{key}$gcosii{$key}:$dirs{$key}:$shells{$key}\n";
};

Paul warns that this script will not move the root account (no problem
there for me) so the output must be appended to the password file.

