Oisin McGuinness wrote:

What I've generally done when a mirror is happening to estimate time is
to do an
iostat rz9 rz17 5
or whatever, where rz9 is the disk being mirrored from, and rz17 is
the disk being mirrored to.
Then after the 1st line or two, one gets values in the bps (blocks per
second) column; a block is 512 bytes, so dividing by two gives
the number of Kbytes/sec being transferred; for a mirroring operation
the reads should match the writes, and dividing the rate into
the disk size gives a rough estimate on the mirror time. I believe that
the write times to the new disk dominate any time spent (if any) in
checking correctness of the data written....

Huseman, Stan at Compaq wrote

You can use the volstat command to gauge progress of an lsm resync -- =
first
get the size of the original member of the lsm plex in blocks, then =
monitor
writes to the newly added plex.  The number of blocks written to the =
new
plex is an approximate indicator.



