From tru64-unix-managers  Thu Jun 15 13:47:52 2000
From: Kim Greer <klg () dec4 ! mc ! duke ! edu>
Date: Thu, 15 Jun 2000 13:47:52 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: automount on DU 4.x
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96107694919970


keywords:  summary howto how-to automount auto.master disk NFS remote

Many thanks for the quick, *helpful* replies from:

	Dr. Tom Blinn
	Sean O'Connell	
	Oisin McGuinness
	Ian Mortimer

Here is a summary of the replies I received concerning
setting up automounting.  The "complete" (trimmed-down)
replies are shown after the summary and original
question.  I hope I haven't botched any attributions.

** Note that this setup is a simple one, limited to doing
"conventional" NFS /etc/fstab-type mounting, except that it
automounts (to avoid any confusion:  /etc/fstab IS NOT USED)
Further note that the "/b" in the AUTOMOUNT_ARGS can be some
other directory name you wish to create.  These instructions
assume the remote disk is not already mounted, say by
conventional "mount" command.  Do NOT have an entry in
_both_ /etc/fstab and /etc/auto.* (just a reminder if you
are switching from "mount" to "automount".)  NIS/YP is not
needed IN THE EXAMPLE SHOWN - IT DOES MATTER OTHERWISE in a
more complicated config.  I will soon investigate the DU <->
Linux parameters and the DU <-> Solaris parameters.  It is
further assumed that remote_hostname:/etc/exports and any
other files, permissions, daemons, etc. are correctly set up
and running.

1. create file /etc/auto.master on the local computer, 
contents (generic names shown):

	/-                      /etc/auto.machine       -intr

2. create file /etc/auto.machine on the local computer,
contents (generic names shown):

	/nfs/remote_host_1/usra          remote_host_1:/usra
	/nfs/remote_host_1/usrb          remote_host_1:/usrb
	#
	/nfs/remote_host_2/usrc          remote_host_2:/usrc
	/nfs/remote_host_2/usrd          remote_host_2:/usrd

3. create directory that mount points will be put under: "mkdir /b"
	("automount" did this for me automatically, I think).

4. with "rcmgr" or manually edit /etc/rc.config

	AUTOMOUNT="1"
	export AUTOMOUNT
	AUTOMOUNT_ARGS="-v -M /b -f /etc/auto.master"
	export AUTOMOUNT_ARGS

5. option 1:
	cd to /sbin/rc3.d
	./S0nfsmount stop;./S20nfsmount start
   option 2:
	reboot

I hope I haven't left out any steps.  My specific problem
was not specifying the *correct* AUTOMOUNT_ARGS in
/etc/rc.config, and not understanding the NIS/YP
interaction.  I did however already have the
"AUTOMOUNT="1"" entry, and did attempt running "automount"
manually, but with wrong arguments.

More Notes:  It should go without saying, but anytime you make
changes to /etc/fstab and-or /etc/auto.[master,machine], you
should first unmount any possibly affected drives.

Thanks again to Tom, Ian, Sean and Oisin, and any others whose
reply comes after composing this summary.

Kim Greer

