=====================================
Reply from Sean O'Connell
=====================================

Here is an example of one of our machines.  Make sure that you have
enabled automounting in /etc/rc.config.

In /etc/rc.config, I add

AUTOMOUNT="1"
AUTOMOUNT_ARGS="-v -M /b -f /etc/auto.master"

and my /etc/auto.master looks like:

# $Id: auto.master,v 1.1 2000/01/01 01:49:40 sto Exp $
#
# mount-point           mapname                 mount-options
#
/usr/home               /etc/auto.home          -intr
/-                      /etc/auto.machine       -intr

and my /etc/auto.home is

# $Id: auto.home,v 1.1 2000/01/01 01:50:31 sto Exp $
#
# key           mount-options           location
#
fac                                     server:/usr/home/&
grad                                    server:/usr/home/&
guest                                   server:/usr/home/&
staff                                   server:/usr/home/&

and my /etc/auto.machine (for machine specific mounts)

# $Id: auto.machine,v 1.2 2000/01/01 02:35:28 sto Exp $
#
# key                   mount-options           location
#
# faculty boxes
/mach1/local                                    mach1:/mach1/local
/mach2/local                                    mach2:/mach2/local
/mach3/local                                    mach3:/mach3/local
#
# web
#
/path/to/apache                               www:/path/to/apache
/path/to/ftp                                  www:/path/to/ftp
#
# misc
#
/usr/local/misc                                 server:/usr/local/misc
/usr/local/src                                  server:/usr/local/src
#
# FreeBSD
#
/FreeBSD/local                                  server:/FreeBSD/local


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
=====================================
Reply from Oisin McGuinness
=====================================

If you read the startup script /sbin/init.d/nfsmount (that in rc3.d just links to this),
the start of automount is conditional on the existence, and values, of two
environment variables, which need to be set in /etc/rc.config.
You could add lines manually to this, or use rcmgr to do it.
>From reading the script:

AUTOMOUNT=1
export AUTOMOUNT
AUTOMOUNT_ARGS="arguments desired for automount" ### e.g, -f /etc/auto.master
export AUTOMOUNT_ARGS

added into /etc/rc.config should do the trick.
 
As the man page for automount hints:

 The automount daemon can be started from the /etc/rc.config file or from
  the command line. 

=====================================
Reply from Ian Mortimer
=====================================
>       Not Running NIS/YP, if that matters

Yes it does.

>       b) made /etc/auto.master, with contents:
>               # mount-point           mapname           mount-options
>               #
>               /net       -hosts
>               /home      auto.indirect     -rw
>               /-         auto.direct       -ro,intr

If your mapname doesn't have a directory path automount looks
for a NIS map of that name.  To have it read the map from
a file you need to specify the full path.  So you need:

        /-         /etc/auto.direct       -ro,intr


