Original post:

Hi -

I changed a group id from 101 to 100.  Now I'm getting a message:

Group id mismatch for /disk01/oradata/newprod/control01.ctl (101, 100)

Is this group id stored internally in the file somehow?  How can I change
it?  I've tried changing (chown) the owner:group to something different and
chowning it back but it didn't help.

Thanks!

Andy
==============================
Solution:

It turns out that you can't chown or chgrp a file that's part of an Oracle
Installation.  If you need to do this you have to shutdown, chown/chmod,
relink the Oracle executable(s).  I just chgrp'ed back to what it was.  
==============================
Summary of replies:

You may want to do a 
find -gname 101 -print

to see if any files are misnumbered
..
What about /etc/passwd ? The groups are assigned by numbers not names so
messing with the group file can screw up the id lookups that the OS does.
I find the utility 'id' handy when messing with such things.
...
You would probably be better off posting the question to an Oracle group.
The
problem is in Oracle --- Unix doesn't save any group information for a file
other than the owning GID for the inode, which chmod changes.
Did you restart the RBDMS after making the changes?

I'm not real good with Oracle, but I recall it requiring you creating an
user
and group for it and providing such to the program during install.  It 
presumably stores that UID and GID all over the place.

Call Oracle or ask an oracle mailing list if they know how to change that
UID/GID after oracle is installed.  IMHO, I'd avoid changing that if at all
possible--- it may very well require an export of the database, reinstall of
oracle, and import of data again.


Thanks alot everyone!
Andy

