My apologies for the delay on this summary.  The solution to controlling
compression came from alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com who said I should monitor
the front-panel lights on the drive and if they don't report the correct
settings, then I should adapt the ddr entry for a DEC TZ88 drive.  My
adapted ddr.dbase entry for a Quantum DLT4000 looks like this:

SCSIDEVICE
     #
     # Matches TZ88, TZ885, TZ887
     #
     Type = tape
     Name = "Quantum" "DLT4000"
     #
     PARAMETERS:
         TypeSubClass        = tk
         TagQueueDepth       = 0
         MaxTransferSize     = 0x0ffffff         # (16MB - 1)
         ReadyTimeSeconds    = 45                # seconds

     DENSITY:
         #
         DensityNumber = 0
         DensityCode = 0x18
         CompressionCode = 0x0
         Buffered = 0x1

     DENSITY:
         #
         DensityNumber = 1
         DensityCode = default
         CompressionCode = 0x1
         Buffered = 0x1

     DENSITY:
         #
         DensityNumber = 2,4,5,6,7
         DensityCode = default
         CompressionCode = 0x0
         Buffered = 0x1

     DENSITY:
         #
         DensityNumber = 3
         DensityCode = 42500_bpi
         CompressionCode = 0x0
         Buffered = 0x1

When I added this entry and compiled a new ddr.db using the command
"/sbin/ddr_config -c" command, the kernel picked up the new settings
without requiring a system reboot.  Now the /dev/nrmt0m device gives me
20GB -compression, and the /dev/nrmt0h device gives me 40GB +compression.

The STU program that Eberhard Heuser-Hofmann talked about looks interesting
for other more exotic operations, though.

On an unrelated note, I was trying to load DU4.0E onto an older
AlphaStation 200 4/100 to test some things when I encountered a pleasant
suprise regarding video support in that rev.  Previously this box had
been loaded with RedHat Linux 5.2 for Alpha, and that had required an
S3 ViRGE chip set video card (Diamond Stealth 3D 2000) running under ARC
console to get anything useful beyond base VGA.  However the DU4.0E could
only handle it using the generic VGA driver.  To my suprise, when I swapped
the Diamond card for a PowerGraph STB card based on the S3 Trio 64V+
chip set, the genvmunix kernel happily accepted it as if it were really
a Digital card.  Now I just have to load the proper driver into the
regular vmunix kernel on this system.  Yippee.


