However, I didn't get very far.  I shut down and put in the new disks
leaving the old disks right where they were.  But, when I tried to
run the Raid configuration utility (RAID Array 230/Plus Software RAID
Configuration Utility) it did not display its main menu; it just gave me 
a message "installation aborted" and told me to reboot.  The old disks are 
in slots 1,3,5.  I tried putting the three new disksin slots 0,2,4 and 
also 2,4,6.

When I took the new disks out leaving only the original disks I could
run the configuration utility just fine.  Kind of a Catch-22.. the
configuration utility will only run if I don't put in the disks I want
to configure?

Any ideas what might be wrong?


----- Original post -----

We have a Storageworks RAID SBB shelf in an Alphaserver 4100
running Digital Unix 4.0E.  It currently has three 4GB disks.
We want to replace the three 4GB disks with three 9GB disks.

The three disks are configured as one logical RAID disk.  At
the Unix level the RAID disk contains one AdvFS file domain
(named "raid1") that contains 4 filesystems (raid1#live, 
raid1#release, raid1#development, raid1#work). ...

----- Representative replies -----

From: Flannon Jackson <flannon@du.edu>
If I were going to do it I would make a new raid set with the 9 gig drives
then do an addvol to add the new raid set to the advfs domain and an rmvol
to remove the 4 gig drives. 

From: "Partin, Kevin S" <Kevin.Partin@SW.Boeing.com>
To keep downtime to a minimum, you could put the 9 GB drives in the three
empty slots in the shelf, configure another 3 disk RAID set. reboot and
rebuild the kernel so that it sees the new raid set. Then use addvol to add
the new RAID set to the existing AdvFS domain, the do rmvol to remove the
original RAID set. AdvFS will move the data from to the new volume and
evacuate the old volume. After the evacuation is complete, shutdown and
unconfigure the old RAID set. Reboot and rebuild one last time and you are
done. No tape involved.

