Hello,

SUMMARY:
------------------------------------------------------------------------
The problem with gdb stepping into a function instead of over with the
gdb next command when used with gcc 2.95.1 and 2.95.2 is a gcc bug
specific to the Alpha.  A fix was added to the development version of
gcc recently.  I have not verified this but I will once the next
release of gcc is available.  A gdb work-around is noted below in Debra
Alpert's response.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

---
Regards,
Richard Jackson
Computer Center Lead Engineer,
Central Systems & Dept. UNIX Consulting
University Computing & Information Systems (UCIS)
George Mason University, Fairfax, Virginia

RESPONDERS:
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Andrew Hobson <ahobson@eng.mindspring.net>
Debra Alpert <alpert@fas.harvard.edu>
Larry Griffith <larry@cs.wsc.ma.edu>
------------------------------------------------------------------------

QUESTION:
------------------------------------------------------------------------
I noted that gdb 4.16, 4.18, and 20000314 all step into gcc 2.95.2
(g++) and gcc 2.95.1 iostream via a next.  However, compiling with gcc
2.8.1 and using gdb does not step into iostream.  Is this a bug or
configuration problem with gcc or gdb?  It seems it maybe a gcc
problem.  We are using AlphaServers running Digital UNIX 4.0D.
However, under Solaris 2.7 with gdb 19990712 and gcc 2.95.2 it works
fine.  I posted this question to gnu.gcc.bug (USENET) and the response
back is 'The debug info being produced by the compiler on your
AlphaServers must be wrong.'  I reported the possible bug to 
gcc-bugs@gcc.gnu.org without a response.

Reduced program to test, test.cpp:
--------------------------------------
#include <iostream>

int main ()
{
   cout << "\nStarting main";
   cout << "\nLeaving main";
   return 0;
}
--------------------------------------

Test to reproduce problem:
g++ -g test.cpp
gdb a.out
break main
run
n
n
q

Does anyone have any suggestions?

This is the gdb session using the students real program:
