RESPONSES:
------------------------------------------------------------------------
>From Andrew Hobson <ahobson@eng.mindspring.net>:
It's a bug in gcc that it just so happens was fixed in the development
version of gcc yesterday.  I'd guess that the gcc 3.0 release will
have this fix in it.

http://gcc.gnu.org/ml/gcc-patches/2000-05/msg01282.html

and

http://gcc.gnu.org/ml/gcc-patches/2000-05/msg01285.html
------------------------------------------------------------------------
>From Debra Alpert <alpert@fas.harvard.edu>:
We noticed a similar problem on our systems. The detailed description is
available at the URL:

http://sourceware.cygnus.com/ml/bug-gdb/1999-10/msg00048.html.

One of the responses,

http://sourceware.cygnus.com/ml/bug-gdb/1999-04/msg00109.html,

suggested patches to two files, gdb/alpha-tdep.c and gdb/symtab.c, that
are part of the gdb-4.17 distribution, as a workaround to the problem.
I've installed these patches and recompiled gdb-4.17. With these fixes,
the problem I described is eliminated. I compiled your little code
snippet, and with the patched gdb, the behavior of next is as expected.
The original version of gdb-4.17, on the other hand, exhibits the aberrant
behavior you noted when applied to a.out.

Various responses to my original post indicate that the underlying problem
is a bug in gcc-2.95.1 which produces bad function prologues that confuse
gdb. The fix is not trivial since it would affect machine independent
parts of gcc as well as the alpha specific parts. It does not seem that
such a fix is forthcoming any time soon. The patch to which I refer does
not rectify the underlying gcc problem, but works around it.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
>From Larry Griffith <larry@cs.wsc.ma.edu>:
I also complained to the bug reporting address for GCC without success. gdb 
also steps into user code even with a next command.  We are using some old DEC 
3000's, so it isn't peculiar to AlphaServers.  My complaint is almost a year 
old, incidentally.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

